branch included) and a quote break added attributes to the user button, the class span and the row. In real Chromium 151 (no content security policy set, so the escaping alone was judged) the head was inert for an img handler, an svg onload, double and single quote breaks with onfocus and autofocus, and a details toggle in every settings, first-run and name sink, where the base executed img and details in each settings and first-run sink and the double quote break in the name and class. The user table row itself could not be driven in Chromium (the page script stops at a top-level error in a bare page) and is covered by the jsdom run only. No fixed sink builds an href or a src from a value. Every value that reaches an attribute in the fixed lines goes through escapeAttrValue; escapeHtml is used only for element content. Text a person reads (an ampersand, an apostrophe, a quoted word, an already-encoded entity) shows exactly as typed at the head, where the base turned an encoded entity back into markup and cut a data-arg at a quote. (3) The cells and the proofs: the new test file holds 20 cells; the review ran the head twice (42 of 42 across both new files) and the new file against the base tree (15 of the 20 cells red, the five that pin unchanged output green); every plant the review applied turned exactly its predicted cells red. (4) The full verify, the local code scan and the hosted code scan check are as in the comment on RD-835 (one pull request): 6128 of 6128 tests across 386 suites, 29 results at base and head with none new, and the hosted check read success with No new alerts in code changed by this pull request. (5) Findings, and what they leave open for this ticket. F1 Minor: settings.js defines escapeHtml twice (lines 93 and 2440); the page binds the second (measured in jsdom and in Chromium 151), which returns a non-string value as its string form without escaping, so a validator field that arrives as an array or an object holding markup is still written