enforced, with production plus the flag behaving token for token like the flag unset; a follow-up is proposed for a committed cell of that kind; (3) Microsoft's real keys, real tokens and key rotation were not exercised; (4) whether the global sign-in check lets Microsoft's webhook reach this check on an instance with sign-in enforced was not run (a follow-up question is proposed). The records entry of this pass names the key of each follow-up that was filed. Code scanning alerts are not claimed closed or changed by this ticket. Nothing is put on a host by this change.
T-445-Detail [d779daf05473] <<LAND234>> This ticket's item 2 (backend/marketplaceJwt.js ~113 accepts any bearer token when MARKETPLACE_SKIP_JWT=true. Confirm no template, compose file, workflow or doc sets it; refuse to honour it when NODE_ENV=production (or remove it), and log loudly when it is on; cell: production + flag -> still verifies), read against the independent review and the builder's record. (1) The change in backend/marketplaceJwt.js: two helpers, skipJwtFlagIsOn (line 65, the flag read on every call, true in any letter case) and isProductionEnv (line 69, the environment name trimmed and lower-cased, equal to production); jwtMiddleware (line 115) logs one error line when it is built with the flag on in production (line 118) and one warning line when it is built with the flag on elsewhere (line 120); inside the request handler (line 134) a flag on in production logs an error line (line 137) and falls through to the unchanged verification (lines 145 to 155), while a flag on elsewhere keeps the old bypass, with its old log line moved into that branch (lines 139 to 141). The three new log strings are fixed ASCII and name only the two variable names; the 401 bodies are unchanged. The header check (line 124 onward) still runs first and the HMAC check in backend/marketplace-api.js (line 82) runs before this check (line 83), so an anonymous caller reaches neither new request-time line. The middleware is built once per process, at that line, inside the Marketplace router, which backend/server.js builds only when the Marketplace setting is on (line 778) and mounts at line 22491. (2) What the review measured, with the real token library and no jest: a fresh node process for each of 77 cells (7 flag values by 11 environment names, each cell sending a self-signed token, a string that is not a JWT, no header and a Basic header). At the head 12 cells skip the check (the flag true or TRUE with NODE_ENV prod, staging, development, test, empty or unset); on main 22 skipped (the same two flag values in all 11 columns). The 10 cells that skipped on main and verify at the head gave answers equal to main's flag-unset production answers (10 of 10); the 55 cells that verified on main gave equal answers at the head (55 of 55); no header and a Basic header gave 401 INVALID_AUTH_HEADER in all 77 cells at both trees. The head wrote an error line in 10 cells (flag on, production forms), a warning line in 12 (flag on, not production) and nothing otherwise. (3) The real library, run outside the test runner by the review (a throwaway RSA key served as a key set from a loopback-only server, every other host refused, one positive control): a token signed by the served key with the right audience and issuer was accepted; a token signed by another key under the same key id, one with the none algorithm, one with a changed payload, a wrong audience, a non-Microsoft issuer, an expired time, an HS256 token keyed with the public key and a string that is not a JWT were each refused 401; the answers were equal at the head with the flag true in production, at the head with the flag unset, and on main with the flag unset; in development with the flag on all nine passed as skipped, the key set fetched 0 times (the kept bypass); with the key set address closed, every token was refused (it fails closed). Established: when the check runs, the real library enforces the signature, the algorithm list, the audience, the expiry and the issuer patterns. Not established: Microsoft's real keys and tokens, key rotation and the cooldown and timeout settings, and Node 24, the version the hosted build uses. (4) The cells and the proofs: the new test file holds 20 cells (R1 to R6 for production with the flag on, B1 to B6 for outside production, F1 to F5 for the flag unset, false or empty, H1 for the header, C1 and C2 for the source); the review ran it twice (20 of 20 each time), and against main's module with the new file (12 green, 8 red: R1 to R6, B6 and C2); of 10 mutants of its own, 8 turned exactly their predicted cells red and 2 informative ones (the flag test widened with trim; the audience option dropped from the verify call) turned none red (finding M-1). The builder reported 12 plants, each turning its derived cells red. (5) The full verify at the merged head read 6148 of 6148 tests across 387 suites, equal to the committed counts (the review, local, Node 26 against the hosted build's Node 24). The review's local code scan against the base read 29 results at base and head, none new, none gone. The hosted code scan check of the head read success: No new alerts in code changed by this pull request; no open alert on main sits in backend/marketplaceJwt.js or backend/marketplace-api.js (22 open in all, 0 in either file). (6) Findings, none blocking. M-1 Minor: the committed cells cannot see a weakened verification, because the library is mocked (stated honestly in the test header, the commit message and the pull request); the real-library proof is the review's own run; a follow-up is proposed. M-2 Minor: cell C2 pins source text (the environment name must occur exactly once in the middleware file), so a harmless refactor that reads it twice would redden it; accepted for this change, the behaviour cells R1 to R6 already carry the property. M-3 Minor: the kit's count of test files was one off (468 and 386 measured at the earlier main, 470 and 388 at the base, 471 and 389 at the head); no product effect. M-4 Minor: the builder's record says an earlier kit was not read whole, the landing rule line was not printed and a chain log was not read; no product effect. I-1 Info: the residual (1) above, measured; the bypass remains outside production exactly as stated. I-2 Info (pre-existing, out of scope, not changed): the middleware's failure log line writes the library's error text, which for an issuer failure includes the token's issuer, but only after a valid signature; the key set cache keeps the first address for the life of the process; the audience and key set address are operator settings. I-3 Info (pre-existing, unmeasured at run time): the review did not run whether the global sign-in check admits the webhook on an instance with sign-in enforced; a follow-up question is proposed. (7) Not claimed, and left open: items 1, 3 and 4 of this ticket; a boot-time refusal; a committed real-library cell; the sign-in check question; code scanning alerts (none claimed closed or changed). Not measured: Microsoft's real keys and tokens, a hosted instance and its settings, Partner Center, Linux and Node 24, and the Deploy demo run a landing starts (it read skipped). [LAND-234-445-20NOV]
