T-NEWE-SUMMARY (238 characters) [b61033ee25c2] The committed cells for RD-445 mock the token library, so a weakened verification (the audience option dropped from the verify call, a widened flag test) stays green; a cell that loads the real library against a loopback key set is wanted
T-NEWE-P1 [933f6d01f9de] BLUF: pull request 234 (RD-445 item 2) refuses the skip flag in production, and its 20 committed cells mock the token library (jose) and the logger, so they prove which path the middleware takes and what it does with the library's answer, not what the library checks. The independent review planted two mutants that stayed 20 of 20 green: one dropped the audience option from the verify call (backend/marketplaceJwt.js line 90) and one widened the flag test so that a padded value would be honoured. The pull request text, the test file's header, the commit message and the builder's record all say the cells mock the library, so nothing is overstated; the gap is that no committed cell would notice a weakened verification. The review closed it for this change by its own run outside the test runner: it loaded the real library in plain node, served a throwaway RSA key as a key set from a loopback-only server on a port the system chose, refused every other host, and sent nine tokens. One signed by the served key with the right audience and issuer was accepted (the positive control) and eight were refused 401 (another key under the same key id, the none algorithm, a changed payload, a wrong audience, a non-Microsoft issuer, an expired time, an HS256 token keyed with the public key, a string that is not a JWT); the answers were equal in production with the flag on, with the flag unset, and on main with the flag unset. Minor, as the review graded it (its finding M-1). It landed on main with pull request 234 (a merge commit of 2026-11-20 08:35 AEDT).
T-NEWE-P2 [4e2f848ac323] Where (read at that merge commit, not run): backend/marketplaceJwt.js requires jose at line 28; getJwks (line 41) builds the remote key set once with createRemoteJWKSet and keeps the first one for the life of the process (the variable at line 39), with a cooldown of 30000 ms and a timeout of 5000 ms (lines 46 and 47); validateMarketplaceToken (line 78) reads the audience from its options, MARKETPLACE_AUDIENCE or the default (line 82) and the key set address from its options, MARKETPLACE_JWKS_URL or the default (line 83), calls the verify function with the audience and a 60 s clock tolerance (lines 90 to 93) and then checks the issuer against two patterns (line 95). The new test file mocks jose with a stub whose verify function accepts one string (lines 13 to 21) and mocks the logger (line 22). Two things make a real-library cell cheap: the audience and the key set address are read on every call, and a plain node process can require the package (the review's program did, with no network but loopback). Because the key set is cached by the first call, a cell that serves its own key set must set the address before the first call in its process (one process for each cell, or a reset of the cache, which the module does not offer today).
T-NEWE-P3 [e1aebf9c19ae] Recommendation (a proposal, not decided): add a cell in the form the review used, a plain-node or ESM-capable cell that loads the real library against a loopback-only key set, in its own process, with the address and the audience set by the cell. A token signed by the served key with the right audience and a Microsoft issuer is accepted; the same token with a wrong audience, an expired time, a non-Microsoft issuer, another key under the same key id, the none algorithm and a changed payload is refused 401; and the same answers hold with MARKETPLACE_SKIP_JWT set to true and NODE_ENV set to production, which is the property RD-445 item 2 needs. A fence that refuses every host but the loopback address keeps the cell off the network. Decide whether it runs under the project's test runner (whether the real package loads there was not tried by anyone in this pass) or as a separate step of the verify command. Cells: each of the review's two mutants (the audience option dropped; the flag test widened) turns at least one cell red; the existing 20 cells pass. Low proposed: the review itself measured the real behaviour for this change, nothing is known to be wrong, and the gap is in what a later edit could silently weaken.
