T-NEWF-SUMMARY (243 characters) [c018dac03849] Does the global sign-in check let Microsoft's Marketplace webhook through on an instance with sign-in enforced? The webhook path is not on the always-public list and the check is applied before the router is mounted (read from source, not run)
T-NEWF-P1 [f9b99bf3941f] BLUF: a question, not a defect claim. The Marketplace SaaS webhook (the routes under /api/marketplace/webhooks/) is answered by backend/marketplace-api.js after two checks: an HMAC signature of the body (line 82) and a Microsoft-signed Bearer token (line 83, the check pull request 234 hardened under RD-445). The independent review of pull request 234 read that the webhook path is not on the server's always-public list (backend/server.js, the list from line 2223; its Marketplace entries are the landing page and /api/marketplace/subscriptions/resolve and /activate, lines 2248 to 2251), that the global sign-in check is applied to every route at line 2905 (the function starts at line 2627) before the Marketplace router is mounted at line 22491, and that only the CSRF check exempts the webhook (the prefix list entry at line 1546, whose comment says authenticity is enforced by the HMAC check). It did not run whether the sign-in check refuses Microsoft's calls on an instance with sign-in enforced (its Info row I-3, unmeasured at run time). The drafter of this ticket read the function: with sign-in enforced and no session cookie, a request to a path under /api/ that is on none of its exemptions (the always-public list, the two agent-polled state routes behind a configured shared value, static assets) reaches line 2727 and is answered 401 AUTH_REQUIRED. If that is what happens to a Microsoft call, the HMAC and token checks never run and subscription events (unsubscribe, suspend, reinstate, change of plan or quantity) do not reach their handlers; if it is not, some other exemption exists that this note did not find. Pre-existing and not changed by pull request 234. It was raised by the review of that pull request, which landed on main as a merge commit of 2026-11-20 08:35 AEDT.
T-NEWF-P2 [19423da9f051] Where (read at that merge commit, not run): the word webhooks is on 2 lines of backend/server.js, 1543 (a comment) and 1546 (the CSRF prefix list entry); backend/marketplace-api.js line 82 mounts the HMAC check and line 83 the token check on /webhooks; the router is built when the Marketplace setting is on and the managed-app setting is off (server.js line 778) and mounted at line 22491 inside a check that the router exists (line 22490), after the global check at line 2905. requireAuth (line 2627) returns next() for the always-public list (line 2629), for the two agent-polled state routes when a shared value is configured (line 2656), for asset prefixes with inert extensions (lines 2669 to 2688) and, when sign-in is positively not enforced, for everything (line 2712, the open window); with sign-in enforced it reads the session cookie (line 2722) and, with none, answers a path under /api/ 401 AUTH_REQUIRED (lines 2724 to 2727). The two Marketplace routes that are public (resolve and activate, lines 2250 and 2251) were added for the landing page that Microsoft's purchase redirect opens (RD-11). The webhook path itself is not in that list, and an always-public entry matches exactly (RD-438), so a path under /webhooks/ would need its own exact entry or a prefix rule.
T-NEWF-P3 [8541a8d6b2b5] Recommendation (a proposal, not decided; the first step is to answer the question by running it): start the server with sign-in enforced and the Marketplace setting on, on a loopback port with no outside network, send a webhook body with a correct HMAC and a Bearer token made against a loopback key set, with no session cookie, and read the status and body. If the sign-in check answers it (401 AUTH_REQUIRED), decide the exemption (an exact-match entry for the webhook routes, in the form of the exact-match set used for the two agent-polled state routes at line 2656, so that the HMAC and token checks carry the authenticity) and add cells for it; one answer is wanted from the product owner: Microsoft's lifecycle webhook must reach the handlers on an enforced instance for subscription events to work, so the proposal is to admit it and rely on the two checks. If a call reaches the HMAC check, name the exemption that lets it through and add a cell that pins it. Cells: an enforced instance with no session cookie, a correct HMAC and a valid loopback-signed token reaches the handler; a wrong HMAC is refused by the HMAC check; a request to another /api/ path is still refused 401 by the sign-in check. Medium proposed because the answer may be that subscription events never reach an enforced instance; Low if an exemption exists.
